00:36:43,982 --> 00:36:46,704
We thought we might do it this summer, but that never materialized.

451
00:36:46,764 --> 00:36:48,245
Anyhow, that's a lot of what I do.

452
00:36:48,265 --> 00:36:51,007
I'm a computer engineer by day.

453
00:36:51,027 --> 00:37:02,914
I have gotten well-versed in understanding the traffic laws and how they apply to bicycling, both in Massachusetts and in other states.

454
00:37:02,954 --> 00:37:05,076
I'm not a lawyer, but I do read the

455
00:37:06,757 --> 00:37:08,838
the words on it and try to understand what they mean.

456
00:37:08,858 --> 00:37:10,139
What else?

457
00:37:10,199 --> 00:37:14,262
Like Patrick, I am a league cycling instructor.

458
00:37:14,823 --> 00:37:25,951
We got that a few years ago, and that basically means that we can be indemnified by the league to do cycle instruction if we follow certain principles.

459
00:37:26,732 --> 00:37:34,158
And what that means is, as I pointed out earlier, we can do things like give cycling instruction to the people at the housing authority.

460
00:37:34,638 --> 00:37:39,599
who might not know some of the issues they face even on an e-bike.

461
00:37:40,499 --> 00:37:42,859
And so I look forward to doing some of that work.

462
00:37:44,140 --> 00:37:50,701
I got on the commission originally because I just, you know, I wanted to really encourage cycling and we've done quite a bit of that.

463
00:37:50,841 --> 00:38:00,863
And it's been a little bit sluggish, but we're making progress, I think, in getting paint on the road in a lot of places and getting the DCR to come around, at least build a few paths.

464
00:38:00,883 --> 00:38:02,103
We've got a clipper ship connector.

465
00:38:03,443 --> 00:38:04,164
that kind of thing.

466
00:38:04,704 --> 00:38:23,453
I get involved with the folks at Mystic River Watershed Association, particularly Amber Christopherson when she's working with paths that they are putting forth, have some contacts with the people in MassDOT and so on.

467
00:38:23,493 --> 00:38:27,235
So I make it kind of my business to poke my nose in a lot of different places.

468
00:38:27,615 --> 00:38:30,597
And that's kind of what I bring to the committee at this point.

469
00:38:32,218 --> 00:38:32,398
Thanks.

470
00:38:33,448 --> 00:38:34,109
Yeah, thanks Bruce.

471
00:38:34,329 --> 00:38:39,493
And I'd say you're, you're probably the ultimate subject matter expert, right?

472
00:38:39,573 --> 00:38:40,373
Cycling related.

473
00:38:41,374 --> 00:38:42,735
So thank you for your expertise.

474
00:38:44,416 --> 00:38:45,237
Uh, Gaston.

475
00:38:47,078 --> 00:38:47,779
Hello everyone.

476
00:38:47,799 --> 00:38:48,420
I'm Gaston.

477
00:38:48,820 --> 00:38:52,883
Um, I like cycling mostly as a means of transportation.

478
00:38:53,303 --> 00:38:56,486
Um, I don't get to commute to work anymore.

479
00:38:57,006 --> 00:38:57,947
Um, and I.

480
00:38:58,918 --> 00:39:02,320
Rarely in these days, I ride a bike recreationally.

481
00:39:02,380 --> 00:39:06,602
I mostly run on trails, either in the woods or in the mountains.

482
00:39:08,544 --> 00:39:19,850
My wife does have to commute to work, so she commutes from here to the south end and back, so she probably bikes actually more than I do these days.

483
00:39:21,071 --> 00:39:23,832
I'm very interested in the environment.

484
00:39:24,333 --> 00:39:28,415
I'm also a member of the Medford Energy and Environment Commission,

485
00:39:29,367 --> 00:39:39,174
And actually here in Massachusetts, transportation accounts for over 40% of the greenhouse gas emissions, which is a lot.

486
00:39:39,254 --> 00:39:40,374
I didn't know it was that high.

487
00:39:41,175 --> 00:39:52,963
And I'm very interested in making it much easier and palatable for people to get interested in human powered means of transportation, particularly biking.

488
00:39:55,604 --> 00:39:59,425
for a lot of things that currently, you know, cars tend to get used.

489
00:40:00,225 --> 00:40:04,306
So, I kind of want to make my focus there.

490
00:40:04,326 --> 00:40:13,209
I'm also very interested in the Complete Streets Committee that MedFar has, how we can interact well with that.

491
00:40:16,370 --> 00:40:20,591
And then I'm a software engineer slash data scientist.

492
00:40:23,163 --> 00:40:34,653
A few years ago already, I did a lot of work in grad school related to demand modeling using tons of data from cell phones and taxis and things like that.

493
00:40:34,713 --> 00:40:37,635
So maybe that will become useful to the group as well.

494
00:40:39,777 --> 00:40:40,037
Great.

495
00:40:40,157 --> 00:40:40,457
Thanks.

496
00:40:41,959 --> 00:40:42,199
Becca.

497
00:40:45,407 --> 00:40:47,991
Hi, I'm Becca Wright.

498
00:40:48,011 --> 00:41:00,307
I have been bike commuting since 2008 when I lived in Los Angeles, and then I moved to Somerville where I lived for nine years and then I just moved to Medford, two years ago.

499
00:41:01,308 --> 00:41:10,217
And I bike now three days a week instead of five days a week from here, Fulton Heights, to I work at MassArt, so Fenway area.

500
00:41:10,978 --> 00:41:15,903
So 18 miles round trip, which is nice to kind of not be remote anymore to get some movement in.

501
00:41:17,704 --> 00:41:23,470
My background is in art and painting, and then I do a webcomic called Bikey Face.

502
00:41:25,145 --> 00:41:30,170
basically try to get everyone on bikes and make it ordinary and acceptable.

503
00:41:30,210 --> 00:41:36,716
So my big thing is breaking down something that seems strange and weird to people and just making it normal.

504
00:41:37,597 --> 00:41:46,146
And I don't know, now that I'm in Medford, I definitely kind of coming from Somerville want to kind of bring Medford kind of more onto bikes.

505
00:41:46,466 --> 00:41:48,508
And so that's why I wanted to join.

506
00:41:50,868 --> 00:41:51,589
Great, thank you.

507
00:41:52,189 --> 00:41:59,255
I was familiar with your cartoon before realizing when I saw your name on the application, I was like, oh, look at that, a celebrity.

508
00:42:00,817 --> 00:42:03,339
Wow, this is actually, this is really cool.

509
00:42:03,359 --> 00:42:06,802
I remember your comic from like years and years and years, and I love that comic.

510
00:42:07,002 --> 00:42:09,144
It's really cool to meet you in person.

511
00:42:09,705 --> 00:42:10,365
Thanks.

512
00:42:11,546 --> 00:42:12,087
Yeah, thank you.

513
00:42:14,889 --> 00:42:16,711
Let's see, Ernie, you're up next.

514
00:42:19,762 --> 00:42:20,402
Hi, everybody.

515
00:42:20,502 --> 00:42:21,683
Yes, Ernie Meunier.

516
00:42:22,063 --> 00:42:27,104
And like Emily, I have Baroque musical roots.

517
00:42:27,805 --> 00:42:40,809
I grew up as a child prodigy, Baroque organist in French Canadian ghettos in northern Rhode Island and, you know, was groomed, sent to seminary and then had to throw out

518
00:42:41,429 --> 00:43:06,708
Catholicism and get involved in drugs in an Ivy League school and learn to become a mechanical engineer and design laboratory equipment and do QA and engineering and got thrown out of that first career and resurfaced and just ended a 38 year career as Boston's independent, the Subaru guru in December.

519
00:43:07,309 --> 00:43:08,390
So as penance,

520
00:43:09,070 --> 00:43:20,016
for putting all those hydrocarbons in the air and stealing, or stealing all that oxygen, I decided that I'd return a bit to my 70s roots and bicycling.

521
00:43:20,036 --> 00:43:22,177
I guess, yeah, I bought that bike at 14.

522
00:43:22,337 --> 00:43:27,400
So I sort of got serious about it eight years ago.

523
00:43:28,181 --> 00:43:35,985
And then eventually, you know, joined the committee, got a couple of bikes, became a weight weenie, which is ridiculous because I'm overweight.

524
00:43:36,764 --> 00:43:45,270
and joined the bike committee and, you know, was struggling with carbon saddles and, you know, was wondering who else was around here.

525
00:43:45,310 --> 00:43:54,296
And I asked Bruce, I said, you know, how long does it take before you get sore on your saddle and need to sort of get out and rest?

526
00:43:54,356 --> 00:43:57,338
And he said, well, around Arizona.

527
00:43:58,257 --> 00:43:59,317
And I'm like, what?

528
00:44:00,397 --> 00:44:04,618
So I realized I was up against some serious people.

529
00:44:04,678 --> 00:44:12,500
And then when Emily said that she was linking together centuries, it sort of blew my mind.

530
00:44:13,380 --> 00:44:17,041
And then my cardiologist said, no, no, you have LBBB.

531
00:44:17,501 --> 00:44:22,322
You go over 135 beats per minute, you may fall off the bike and never get up.

532
00:44:22,802 --> 00:44:26,483
So yeah, I get out of my bike every day.

533
00:44:29,104 --> 00:44:30,005
It's been kind of fun.

534
00:44:30,025 --> 00:44:39,652
And the inspiration now is that I have grandkids, you know, five, nine and 13, who is starting to bike, etc.

535
00:44:40,293 --> 00:44:43,896
So it's less about my children, who are adults.

536
00:44:44,797 --> 00:44:59,414
And I'm having a blast with this bike lights thing because I think that getting kids involved with their first bikes and getting excited about it is actually easier than dealing with teenagers or adults.

537
00:45:00,495 --> 00:45:09,320
or especially an infrastructure that has such inertia around it, that's fun.

538
00:45:10,581 --> 00:45:25,950
I'm trying to give back in that way and help foment the transition toward human-powered transportation and electric vehicles as well, just to move this along.

539
00:45:28,712 --> 00:45:29,352
Maybe that's it.

540
00:45:30,207 --> 00:45:30,487
Thanks.

541
00:45:31,869 --> 00:45:32,089
Great.

542
00:45:32,309 --> 00:45:32,849
Thanks, Ernie.

543
00:45:33,410 --> 00:45:35,472
Well, that's everyone on the commission now.

544
00:45:35,692 --> 00:45:38,895
Thanks to the folks who've been here a long time.

545
00:45:39,015 --> 00:45:40,236
Thanks to the new people.

546
00:45:40,757 --> 00:45:51,126
We've got a lot of diverse experiences and probably pet issues that we care about, and that's very helpful, I think, for making the commission stronger.

547
00:45:51,746 --> 00:45:53,048
Look forward to working with you all.

548
00:45:53,975 --> 00:45:59,059
I think we have another guest who has joined at SIGGRAPH.

549
00:45:59,719 --> 00:46:00,059
Oh, yeah.

550
00:46:00,500 --> 00:46:00,840
Hi, Matt.

551
00:46:00,980 --> 00:46:02,101
I believe you've joined us before.

552
00:46:02,121 --> 00:46:02,761
Want to say hi?

553
00:46:03,542 --> 00:46:03,782
Hi.

554
00:46:03,982 --> 00:46:04,563
Hi, everyone.

555
00:46:04,963 --> 00:46:06,724
Yeah, I joined a little late.

556
00:46:07,204 --> 00:46:10,407
I wasn't really... We're just giving our backgrounds generally.

557
00:46:10,427 --> 00:46:11,387
Yeah, this is my second meeting.

558
00:46:11,447 --> 00:46:12,128
Happy to be here.

559
00:46:14,532 --> 00:46:18,936
So yeah, I moved into Medford last year from Somerville.

560
00:46:18,956 --> 00:46:23,200
I've been biking around Cambridge and Somerville since about 2014.

561
00:46:24,281 --> 00:46:28,866
I, until 2020, commuted every day into downtown Boston.

562
00:46:28,906 --> 00:46:34,110
Now I commute into downtown Boston two or three days a week.

563
00:46:34,811 --> 00:46:37,573
I'm not much of a distance biker.

564
00:46:37,674 --> 00:46:39,155
I'm pretty much just a commuter biker.

565
00:46:41,220 --> 00:46:45,163
but I really wanted to get involved in this once I started biking around the Medford roads.

566
00:46:45,823 --> 00:46:55,570
It's just a couple of miles further, but the infrastructure is really, really wanting compared to Somerville and Cambridge, and we're gonna all work on it, of course.

567
00:46:57,151 --> 00:46:59,253
So I wanted to get involved in that respect.

568
00:46:59,753 --> 00:47:09,240
I have a legal background, I'm a municipal attorney, so hopefully I can help in some way in that respect, but I'm just here as a biker, yeah.

569
00:47:10,832 --> 00:47:11,032
Great.

570
00:47:11,132 --> 00:47:12,413
Thanks for coming to the meetings, Matt.

571
00:47:12,433 --> 00:47:13,073
Hope you keep coming.

572
00:47:14,674 --> 00:47:23,360
Well, I can give a bit of a background on the commission itself, just a bit of a primer, like I mentioned, that may be helpful to some of the new folks who may have

573
00:47:24,251 --> 00:47:31,893
different levels of experience with this sort of organization, but it's probably a good idea to get some of that out of the way so that we're all on the same page generally.

574
00:47:33,213 --> 00:47:42,836
So the Medford Bicycle Advisory Commission, we have some of the founding members on the call, as they mentioned, but this is technically an ad hoc commission.

575
00:47:44,196 --> 00:47:50,099
which means that this is not a commission that was established by charter, by the city charter.

576
00:47:50,700 --> 00:47:56,983
Some of the commissions, Gaston, if I'm not mistaken, the energy environment one, I think is by charter, maybe I'm wrong.

577
00:47:57,083 --> 00:47:57,923
Yeah, I think it is.

578
00:47:59,884 --> 00:48:02,886
Those require the swearing in and whatnot.

579
00:48:02,906 --> 00:48:04,747
This one's a little bit different in that regard.

580
00:48:04,847 --> 00:48:10,990
But that said, we are still appointed by the mayor and we've

581
00:48:11,628 --> 00:48:13,730
serve at her pleasure, essentially.

582
00:48:14,811 --> 00:48:20,157
So we are a group that does provide an official function to the city.

583
00:48:20,257 --> 00:48:24,421
So we're not a separate nonprofit advocacy group.

584
00:48:25,442 --> 00:48:32,589
We might advocate for things and we might want a lot of the same things that some of the advocacy groups do and our interests certainly align.

585
00:48:33,130 --> 00:48:34,851
But technically, you know, we are

586
00:48:35,905 --> 00:48:43,314
an official organization associated with the city, so it's good to mind that difference and be cognizant of that fact.

587
00:48:44,997 --> 00:48:52,246
We are governed by Massachusetts law, so there's a couple of key ones that are probably worth knowing about.

588
00:48:54,094 --> 00:48:57,757
One is a conflict of interest law for municipal employees.

589
00:48:58,118 --> 00:49:06,645
That is one where we are technically classified as municipal employees, which may not make any sense because we're not going to be sending you a paycheck, unfortunately.

590
00:49:07,766 --> 00:49:12,069
But under Massachusetts law, people who are involved in their cities in this way,

591
00:49:14,246 --> 00:49:22,733
map, you may know this, and be able to explain it may better than I can, but it's something that I've come across at my at my work that we are classified as municipal employees.

592
00:49:23,753 --> 00:49:33,541
So there are conflict of interest rules like, just be mindful that in some circumstances you may be considered kind of a representative of the city and employee of the city essentially so

593
00:49:34,181 --> 00:49:41,853
There's rules associated with that regarding self-dealing, nepotism and whatnot, bribes, that sort of thing.

594
00:49:41,873 --> 00:49:43,195
They're fairly straightforward.

595
00:49:44,157 --> 00:49:48,984
If you have questions about that sort of thing, the city has a solicitor, their attorney,

596
00:49:49,625 --> 00:49:51,325
that can help us answer those questions.

597
00:49:51,345 --> 00:49:59,007
And we sometimes go to her to help us figure out if there's something that we're doing, if it's appropriate and in line with Massachusetts law and whatnot.

598
00:49:59,267 --> 00:50:07,670
Jared, I think at some point, we're all supposed to take some sort of conflict of interest law training and get a certificate or that kind of thing.

599
00:50:07,710 --> 00:50:08,650
It's pretty straightforward.

600
00:50:08,710 --> 00:50:08,830
But

601
00:50:09,650 --> 00:50:15,332
As I recall, it was one of these questions where they ask you the question and it's like almost common sense.

602
00:50:15,913 --> 00:50:20,295
It's like, so if you're on the commission, can you take money from so-and-so to do this and that?

603
00:50:20,315 --> 00:50:20,955
It's like, no.

604
00:50:21,815 --> 00:50:22,015
Right.

605
00:50:23,376 --> 00:50:23,796
Exactly.

606
00:50:24,016 --> 00:50:27,878
I don't know if the solicitor will mention that to us at some point.

607
00:50:28,938 --> 00:50:36,863
Um, I'll, I'll reach out about that and just, uh, get, I think they, um, send us a packet into on their own.

608
00:50:36,883 --> 00:50:43,027
Um, last time, like sometime last year or something like that.

609
00:50:43,788 --> 00:50:45,809
And I'll imagine that again with new people.

610
00:50:47,550 --> 00:50:48,431
Yeah, that's good to know.

611
00:50:48,491 --> 00:50:55,035
Did I hear someone else that sounded like it was breaking up or are you going to mention.

612
00:50:59,340 --> 00:51:01,561
We hear you, Dennis, but just barely.

613
00:51:01,581 --> 00:51:07,403
All right, well, he probably had something to mention on that front, but we'll put a pin in that until he comes back.

614
00:51:07,463 --> 00:51:09,224
The other one was the open meeting law.

615
00:51:09,364 --> 00:51:11,945
That was another one that I wanted to chat about that governs.

616
00:51:12,805 --> 00:51:17,107
That is really kind of a big thing that determines a lot of how we're allowed to behave.

617
00:51:18,268 --> 00:51:33,094
If you haven't heard about it before, Massachusetts Open Meeting Law is essentially, there's a lot of rules associated with it, but generally the idea is that meetings of public bodies like us have to be done in a public forum.

618
00:51:33,194 --> 00:51:36,015
There's a lot more to it than that, but that's kind of the general sense.

619
00:51:37,739 --> 00:51:44,165
There's a training that they hold, Massachusetts holds trainings on this a couple times a month.

620
00:51:44,766 --> 00:51:48,429
So that's something that anyone who has not done one of those should definitely do it.

621
00:51:48,649 --> 00:51:50,571
And I'll send her on that meeting because it's.

622
00:51:51,328 --> 00:51:59,450
There's a lot of rules associated with it that are not as commonsensical as the one that Bruce mentioned before, the conflict of interest rules.

623
00:52:00,551 --> 00:52:08,853
So it's really worth looking at that training and taking it to heart and paying attention to it because it will explain a lot about the mechanisms of how this commission has to work.

624
00:52:10,119 --> 00:52:22,204
So if there's essentially a quorum of us meeting, so 50% plus one, it needs to be in the public, it needs to be publicly advertised and accessible, posted to the city calendar.

625
00:52:22,244 --> 00:52:25,145
There's a few places where things like that have to be posted.

626
00:52:26,105 --> 00:52:29,707
That's what the secretary, what Lisa has been doing in this case.

627
00:52:30,807 --> 00:52:42,636
So whether that is our monthly meetings or whether it's working group meetings, if we have a smaller group that wants to get together about something, all of those meetings have to be public.

628
00:52:42,676 --> 00:52:46,819
We're not allowed to deliberate about things privately.

629
00:52:48,240 --> 00:52:57,547
Anything, any decisions that we're making need to be happening in an above board way in front of the public and the public needs to be aware of it.

630
00:52:58,507 --> 00:52:59,188
That really comes.

631
00:53:00,266 --> 00:53:16,485
Sorry, yeah, so is that is that only for deliberative actions because, you know, there are things like, you know, I'm like, is like, you know, for information or, you know, clarification, things like that.

632
00:53:18,166 --> 00:53:20,547
Yeah, it's largely deliberative.

633
00:53:21,188 --> 00:53:22,808
That's the main function of that.

634
00:53:22,888 --> 00:53:29,971
Administrative things like when meetings are happening, here's some background information for you to review before the next meeting, that sort of thing.

635
00:53:31,432 --> 00:53:35,774
It's described in those trainings, but that's the kind of thing that is okay.

636
00:53:35,934 --> 00:53:39,815
It's, I have an opinion on topic X, what do you think about it?

637
00:53:40,436 --> 00:53:43,517
That's explicitly the kind of thing that we don't do.

638
00:53:44,838 --> 00:54:00,592
particularly with email is an area that we have to be careful not to step over that bound, because it's so easy to start responding to someone to say, well, I disagree, we should do X, Y and Z. And we have to just cut it off of the knees at that point, so you will take it up at the meeting.

639
00:54:01,694 --> 00:54:03,336
Right, exactly right, Bruce.

640
00:54:03,456 --> 00:54:16,150
Emails are really challenging in that regard, because the thing is, we have the Google groups, whatever it is, it's a long email address that is the email address that sends an email to all of us who are on the commission and a couple of other people.

641
00:54:16,210 --> 00:54:22,737
But that's kind of the way that we remind ourselves, you know, give announcements or send administrative things around.

642
00:54:24,779 --> 00:54:26,840
we can't deliberate over email.

643
00:54:27,000 --> 00:54:38,963
And the thing is that deliberating about a topic that it falls within the purview of our commission is a much broader thing than you might expect it to be.

644
00:54:39,663 --> 00:54:51,767
So having a debate about where some bike lanes should be, I've got an opinion about this, those are all things that as frustrating as it is, we shouldn't be doing any of that over email because

645
00:54:52,927 --> 00:55:00,873
on that email listserv that we have, that group email, it's essentially a quorum all the time, right?

646
00:55:00,973 --> 00:55:12,501
So you send an email, boom, you've got 100% of the commission on it, which is fundamentally means that we can talk about the same things in the same way that we are at this meeting right now, which is exactly what we're not supposed to do.

647
00:55:13,102 --> 00:55:19,707
My understanding is that even in some of those emails, I think I saw you there, Ernie, but just one second.

648
00:55:23,001 --> 00:55:41,768
If I were to deliver an email to the group expressing an opinion about something that falls within our purview, that is generally the type of thing that we would expect to be talking about on this meeting, even if no one responds and has a counter opinion, that already is a problem.

649
00:55:42,455 --> 00:55:51,360
So that's why we just don't talk that much over email, as unhelpful as it, or as challenging as it can be to get things done.

650
00:55:51,981 --> 00:55:53,542
You also can't do workarounds either.

651
00:55:54,042 --> 00:56:10,252
So I can't send an opinion email or a directive to 10 different people separately, knowing that there's not a quorum of people on a given email thread, because I'm clearly just trying to circumvent those rules and still express opinions to all of the same people.

652
00:56:11,352 --> 00:56:12,252
You can't do that either.

653
00:56:12,713 --> 00:56:15,073
The training is helpful because it helps explain some of that stuff.

654
00:56:16,073 --> 00:56:16,934
Ernie, go for it.

655
00:56:16,954 --> 00:56:18,354
You answered one question.

656
00:56:18,414 --> 00:56:27,677
The second one, is there a workable loophole though when we put together ad hoc working groups?

657
00:56:28,497 --> 00:56:28,777
Yes.

658
00:56:29,257 --> 00:56:37,919
To deliberate and then use e-mail freely within our group and then report back formally at an MBAC meeting.

659
00:56:38,560 --> 00:56:39,920
Those are smaller than quorum.

660
00:56:40,549 --> 00:56:43,491
Yeah, as long as they are smaller than quorum.

661
00:56:43,571 --> 00:56:48,675
Smaller than quorum, I see, so n less than seven, okay.

662
00:56:49,116 --> 00:56:49,296
Yeah.

663
00:56:49,436 --> 00:56:49,796
Thank you.

664
00:56:50,276 --> 00:56:50,737
Yep, sure.

665
00:56:51,317 --> 00:56:52,058
Can we use those?

666
00:56:53,019 --> 00:56:55,180
How many people are on the commission in total?

667
00:56:55,280 --> 00:56:56,581
N over two minus one.

668
00:56:58,823 --> 00:57:02,526
Yeah, there's, as of today, there's 13, so our quorum is seven.

669
00:57:02,926 --> 00:57:04,047
So six, good.

670
00:57:04,848 --> 00:57:04,988
Yeah.

671
00:57:07,006 --> 00:57:08,887
So that's something to remember.

672
00:57:09,067 --> 00:57:14,709
Even Google Docs is even a problematic thing in some cases, as useful as that is.

673
00:57:14,729 --> 00:57:20,770
If you have one document that is about some bicycle-related policy, all of us get access to it.

674
00:57:20,911 --> 00:57:24,872
All of us start commenting, replying to one another, and comment threads on the side.

675
00:57:25,332 --> 00:57:28,793
Boom, you've already crossed the line and you are now deliberating about things.

676
00:57:29,567 --> 00:57:35,612
So it is a pain, and it makes our work slower, but that's what we do.

677
00:57:35,632 --> 00:57:39,415
We're an official organization, so we follow the law.

678
00:57:42,177 --> 00:57:42,577
Let's see.

679
00:57:44,397 --> 00:58:04,612
Other things to think about, meetings, as you can tell this is a less formal meeting than what you might expect to see and, you know, city council or something like that where they're generally following Roberts rules of order, the, the official motion seconded, you know, the official rules that make a public meeting sound so so formal.

680
00:58:06,212 --> 00:58:08,853
That's kind of subject to the discretion of the chair.

681
00:58:09,113 --> 00:58:14,315
The chair has the ability to tighten that down and make it as formal as they care to make it.

682
00:58:15,995 --> 00:58:17,876
There is certainly benefits to doing that.

683
00:58:17,936 --> 00:58:25,578
We've generally been a small and polite enough group that that hasn't really been something that we've done as long as I've been on the commission.

684
00:58:27,098 --> 00:58:30,259
But in general, orderliness is good, of course.

685
00:58:31,499 --> 00:58:33,879
In Zoom, it's particularly kind of a challenge, right?

686
00:58:34,340 --> 00:58:43,221
We all have to kind of be mindful of trying to not talk over one another and pay attention to others and be considerate in sharing the time and the space and that sort of thing.

687
00:58:43,281 --> 00:58:46,742
So the chair, again, has the ability to tighten it down.

688
00:58:47,562 --> 00:58:49,562
We're usually pretty casual about it.

689
00:58:51,623 --> 00:58:52,143
Let's see.

690
00:58:53,993 --> 00:58:57,436
Yeah, I mentioned, we sometimes have, Ernie, you mentioned this, but subcommittee meetings.

691
00:58:57,516 --> 00:58:59,797
So that's something that we haven't done a lot of lately.

692
00:59:01,018 --> 00:59:11,266
But we sometimes will break off into smaller groups if they're into kind of little working groups, if there's a particular project or something that a few people have an interest or the inclination to work on that doesn't

693
00:59:11,985 --> 00:59:16,769
that allows us to make progress on a particular project outside of the context of these meetings.

694
00:59:16,849 --> 00:59:21,713
These meetings happen once a month, fourth Wednesday of the month, they're two hours long.

695
00:59:22,093 --> 00:59:24,435
You can only get so much done in that amount of time.

696
00:59:25,255 --> 00:59:28,418
Sometimes those working groups are really useful to make progress in the meantime.

697
00:59:31,100 --> 00:59:32,161
But I think you've heard the mission of

698
00:59:35,259 --> 00:59:41,423
What people have described as wanting to accomplish in the city is kind of in line with our commission goals, right?

699
00:59:41,443 --> 00:59:44,925
I mean, we're basically here to help make cycling better in the city.

700
00:59:45,225 --> 00:59:46,826
So that's what we're doing.

701
00:59:48,187 --> 00:59:51,930
Some of the specific functions that we've done to accomplish that.

702
00:59:53,888 --> 00:59:58,931
several folks in here put huge amounts of hours into the bike master plan.

703
01:00:00,072 --> 01:00:08,416
I think that was maybe just being finalized or had recently been finalized when I came on to the commission.

704
01:00:08,436 --> 01:00:12,258
If you haven't seen that, that's a big plan of

705
01:00:13,679 --> 01:00:28,012
It lays out a page kind of per road section of kind of key parts of the city with kind of a menu of good, better, best options for what the commission thought would be good upgrades to those roads.

706
01:00:28,493 --> 01:00:30,194
So it's kind of depending on the extent of any

707
01:00:30,895 --> 01:00:38,484
renovation and repair work that's being done in a given stretch of road, it kind of gives the city a roadmap for, okay, well, we're just doing very light work.

708
01:00:38,724 --> 01:00:40,406
Here's something that we can easily change.

709
01:00:40,966 --> 01:00:50,397
If we're ripping up the whole road and making something all new, new pavement, whatever, maybe there's a more substantial redesign that's recommended as a part of that process.

710
01:00:51,146 --> 01:00:57,072
So it's a very thorough and helpful document due for a bit of a refresh.

711
01:00:57,413 --> 01:01:00,076
And we actually have the support from the city to do that.

712
01:01:00,316 --> 01:01:05,702
They've hired an intern that will be overseen by the city engineer.

713
01:01:06,522 --> 01:01:09,364
who will work with us to update that document.

714
01:01:09,425 --> 01:01:22,074
So it'll be nice because that means that there will be official city engineering capacity behind that effort, and it's not going to be something that solely rests with an outside volunteer body.

715
01:01:22,174 --> 01:01:35,745
As you can imagine, it's, you know, with a group like us, as well-intentioned as we can be, it's difficult to make the same level of progress that official employees can make with, you know, a work week and 40 hours every week and whatnot.

716
01:01:38,272 --> 01:01:44,746
Let's see other things you know we provide feedback to the city we work with the planning and transportation department engineering department.

717
01:01:46,112 --> 01:01:47,674
to provide feedback on road designs.

718
01:01:47,774 --> 01:02:02,046
So whenever they're doing construction projects, we try to be involved in that process and provide feedback about what we want to see, tweaks to their designs, make recommendations about parts of the road or parts of the city that they should be focusing on that we don't think they are.

719
01:02:02,766 --> 01:02:07,490
Maybe that's based on our own experience or public feedback that we've solicited along the way.

720
01:02:09,532 --> 01:02:12,854
We get called on to attend various public meetings as well.

721
01:02:13,494 --> 01:02:27,601
So there's traffic commission meetings which are actually a lot more important than you may realize, that's kind of a hidden commission that meets on Tuesdays in the late afternoon doesn't sound particularly bicycle related.

722
01:02:28,061 --> 01:02:30,362
Because a lot of what they do is kind of parking related.

723
01:02:31,102 --> 01:02:46,485
But anytime the city wants to do something that is bike related that touches cars, it falls under the traffic commission's purview or not cars rather, but parking, like the removal of parking, the traffic commission has to has to approve it.

724
01:02:47,225 --> 01:02:50,866
So there's a lot of other kind of groups that we will

725
01:02:51,799 --> 01:02:58,144
that we'll deal with in that regard, and we might provide feedback from our personal or commission perspective on some of those meetings.

726
01:02:59,125 --> 01:03:04,730
We've attended city council meetings, council of the whole, meeting of the whole, what is it, council of the whole?

727
01:03:05,010 --> 01:03:05,750
Why does that sound wrong?

728
01:03:05,770 --> 01:03:06,231
Is that right?

729
01:03:07,172 --> 01:03:08,353
Committee of the whole, that's it.

730
01:03:08,853 --> 01:03:10,735
Right, there we go.

731
01:03:11,835 --> 01:03:19,201
So we provide kind of subject matter expertise on bike-related issues for those sorts of meetings, for city officials who may not be experts themselves,

732
01:03:21,033 --> 01:03:34,542
A really nice development that is that the city now has dedicated bike related officials essentially and engineers, so they have people who are involved in the engineering department now.

733
01:03:37,860 --> 01:03:41,402
actually care about making biking better.

734
01:03:41,422 --> 01:03:52,907
In the past, there just weren't necessarily specific people tasked with that, but now they have the expertise, they have the interest, the department exists.

735
01:03:52,947 --> 01:03:56,108
In the past, I think we were a little more isolated from that process.

736
01:03:56,228 --> 01:03:57,789
Sometimes we would find out about things

737
01:03:58,589 --> 01:04:09,815
a little too late to be that effective at changing them in some cases just because we were kind of the separate body but it feels like we've been embraced into the city's planning department.

738
01:04:10,563 --> 01:04:17,365
I dare say, Jared, that that's something that the bike commission at least provided some impetus to the city to hire people.

739
01:04:17,566 --> 01:04:24,288
And the current DPW director, previously the city engineer, Tim McGivern, was on the commission.

740
01:04:24,388 --> 01:04:26,849
So we definitely have a friendly voice there.

741
01:04:27,569 --> 01:04:29,890
And then we also ended up getting Todd Blake,

742
01:04:30,610 --> 01:04:34,793
as another person who was brought on board who was actually very bike-friendly as well.

743
01:04:35,414 --> 01:04:41,018
And I think that was partly because we were lobbying the city to move in that direction.

744
01:04:41,799 --> 01:04:43,180
Yeah, great point, Bruce.

745
01:04:43,300 --> 01:04:43,800
Absolutely.

746
01:04:44,060 --> 01:04:45,722
And it has really paid off.

747
01:04:46,582 --> 01:04:47,683
Ernie, go for it.

748
01:04:47,723 --> 01:04:54,609
Which of the three, including Angie, Todd's assistant, is directly involved in the master plan intercourse?

749
01:04:56,113 --> 01:04:57,974
Good question, Ernie.

750
01:04:59,254 --> 01:05:05,096
I can run down some of those key people that we've mentioned here for those who may not be as familiar, but there's Tim McGivern.

751
01:05:05,676 --> 01:05:07,076
He used to be on the bike commission.

752
01:05:07,717 --> 01:05:10,477
I think he was one of the original members, if I'm not mistaken.

753
01:05:11,998 --> 01:05:16,099
He's the commissioner of public works.

754
01:05:16,639 --> 01:05:18,340
He's the head honcho there, which is terrific.

755
01:05:21,258 --> 01:05:26,141
Let's see, Todd Blake, he's the director of traffic and transportation.

756
01:05:26,261 --> 01:05:32,365
He is involved in a lot of the infrastructure type improvements that we'd like to see in the city.

757
01:05:33,386 --> 01:05:38,209
Amy Ingalls, she is the transportation engineer.

758
01:05:38,390 --> 01:05:40,991
Todd got promoted and they brought on a new engineer.

759
01:05:41,472 --> 01:05:44,374
Amy is a local cyclist as well.

760
01:05:44,514 --> 01:05:45,775
She bikes through the area.

761
01:05:45,795 --> 01:05:47,476
She attends a lot of our meetings.

762
01:05:47,676 --> 01:05:50,438
Todd does too, but Amy has been attending pretty much all of them lately.

763
01:05:51,679 --> 01:05:55,781
which is great, and she's a very friendly voice to cycling issues in Medford.

764
01:05:56,642 --> 01:06:09,011
And the two of them, Todd and Amy, really control a lot of the paint that goes on the ground in a lot of ways, so it's really nice that they are, that they involve us in the process and seek our input.

765
01:06:09,932 --> 01:06:16,056
I also just read that the city appointed a new city engineer, I guess, to take Tim's place.

766
01:06:16,737 --> 01:06:18,918
I don't recall the name of the person, however.

767
01:06:18,938 --> 01:06:19,979
Oh, great.

768
01:06:21,373 --> 01:06:24,016
another person to rope into our meetings, perhaps.

769
01:06:25,538 --> 01:06:37,494
Since we have bike friendly people, you know, on the various like traffic and whatever, have we done any sort of bike volume studies so that we know like,

770
01:06:38,625 --> 01:06:49,897
Where the bikes are going in Medford, like what streets there on and things like, I mean, we can certainly like, you know, do Dana mining on like Strava and things like that, but not recently.

771
01:06:50,538 --> 01:06:53,101
We've so we've looked at Strava.

772
01:06:55,343 --> 01:07:06,716
And Strava is, I don't remember where we got with that, but there's like a weird thing with Strava where it tends to be a certain segment of bikers who use it.

773
01:07:08,257 --> 01:07:09,559
Yeah.

774
01:07:09,639 --> 01:07:14,003
We did used to, um, do manual bike counts.

775
01:07:14,164 --> 01:07:14,344
Right.

776
01:07:15,184 --> 01:07:17,626
where we would have volunteers out on different intersections.

777
01:07:18,826 --> 01:07:29,772
That was interesting, but it's a lot of work and it can be very weather dependent because you do it one day every six months or maybe once every year.

778
01:07:29,792 --> 01:07:34,134
You're comparing year to year, but the weather is not the same.

779
01:07:34,414 --> 01:07:37,235
The other stuff that's on this schedule is not the same.

780
01:07:37,255 --> 01:07:39,977
But what we've also

781
01:07:43,865 --> 01:07:56,049
A conclusion that we've come to a number of times in some of these volume discussions is it's not necessarily only about where people are going now, it's about where people want to go.

782
01:07:57,270 --> 01:08:04,832
We basically know where people want to go on bikes because it's the same places they want to go every other way.

783
01:08:06,813 --> 01:08:13,734
In some cases, it seems like that would be good useful data to have.

784
01:08:13,894 --> 01:08:17,355
But on the other hand, Medford's not that big.

785
01:08:17,415 --> 01:08:19,335
It doesn't have that many through routes.

786
01:08:20,215 --> 01:08:22,916
We know exactly where people want to go.

787
01:08:24,056 --> 01:08:29,817
If you look at which roads get the most congested, those are the routes that most people are interested in taking.

788
01:08:29,837 --> 01:08:32,117
Then there's also the argument of

789
01:08:35,637 --> 01:08:39,299
you don't judge demand for a bridge by the number of swimmers in the river.

790
01:08:42,680 --> 01:08:50,304
You don't assume that because nobody's biking on a certain road, then nobody's interested.

791
01:08:50,384 --> 01:08:57,547
A great example is like Mystic Avenue, the great straight shot, and those who are comfortable will use it.

792
01:08:58,309 --> 01:09:02,414
Yeah.

793
01:09:03,075 --> 01:09:12,246
So we have done those counts, but that data is difficult to collect and not actually as useful as it should be.

794
01:09:14,240 --> 01:09:15,140
Ernie, I see you there.

795
01:09:15,281 --> 01:09:31,069
Yeah, we've come to sort of the conclusion, because exactly what Emily and Bruce were saying, that maybe it's more like if we build it, they will come, you know, the Apple model, because, you know, High Street is the same thing.

796
01:09:31,129 --> 01:09:32,089
It's too dangerous.

797
01:09:32,149 --> 01:09:33,630
There's not enough help.

798
01:09:34,050 --> 01:09:35,871
Going east-west is a problem.

799
01:09:36,132 --> 01:09:37,192
People want to do it.

800
01:09:37,412 --> 01:09:40,094
There aren't a lot of bikes doing it, and we know why.

801
01:09:40,954 --> 01:09:46,078
So we have to get the infrastructure up to snuff, and we think people will then come.

802
01:09:46,978 --> 01:09:54,284
So that data mining has proved, yeah, somewhat useful, but really biased.

803
01:09:54,524 --> 01:10:05,492
It also would be, even if we had the data that isn't really possible to get, but if we had the data of where every person on a bike is going,

804
01:10:06,694 --> 01:10:07,395
all the time.

805
01:10:07,415 --> 01:10:22,251
If you're doing a bike count out in Medford Square, for example, you will miss all of the people who are taking some circuitous back roads way to avoid Medford Square, even though their route would

806
01:10:24,033 --> 01:10:28,419
ultimately would actually take them through there if they wanted to go that way.

807
01:10:29,761 --> 01:10:39,173
If there's a bad intersection and you want to see how many people are biking through that intersection, you can count them, but you'll miss all the people who know some back way to avoid that intersection.

808
01:10:40,267 --> 01:10:45,853
And another thing on top of that is blue bikes, blue bikes.

809
01:10:45,893 --> 01:10:50,898
Yeah, that's what it's called now blue bikes, those are actually going to be coming to the city sometime this year.

810
01:10:51,439 --> 01:10:56,284
Yes, we've got three stations definitely and more are likely coming.

811
01:10:57,545 --> 01:11:02,810
So part of that process is that there's usually like a data sharing arrangement or something where

812
01:11:04,092 --> 01:11:06,133
we might be able to get access to some kind of data.

813
01:11:06,213 --> 01:11:12,158
Well, they'll at least be able to provide some sense of, you know, which stations are being well utilized and not.

814
01:11:12,218 --> 01:11:15,781
And, you know, Motivate has the company that runs it.

815
01:11:15,821 --> 01:11:18,584
They have an incentive, obviously, to put stations in the right place.

816
01:11:18,644 --> 01:11:21,226
So there is a lot of like kind of data analysis going.

817
01:11:21,586 --> 01:11:22,907
Yes, there is a blue bikes station, like 200 feet.

818
01:11:29,545 --> 01:11:31,386
from my office.

819
01:11:32,507 --> 01:11:32,947
There you go.

820
01:11:33,187 --> 01:11:34,888
Well, there will now be one closer to your house.

821
01:11:37,109 --> 01:11:47,655
One more point about bike counts is that, yeah, like, like, we kind of came to the conclusion like that we had questions about how valid the data was, but

822
01:11:48,930 --> 01:12:09,635
There's, aside from that, there is, especially in the early days, there's real value in getting out on the streets and just seeing, like, for the people doing the counts, like just having that chance to observe traffic for a couple hours, and then taking the data that we did get and presenting it so that we could start making the case and

823
01:12:10,708 --> 01:12:19,912
we could have some data behind a statement like, you know, College Ave is the, like, busiest, like, bike route in the city.

824
01:12:20,552 --> 01:12:38,280
So from that perspective, it had a lot of value and was, and I think, you know, in the future, we may consider doing, like, some spot counts for something if we're, like, trying to make a point, like, this is why we need to, you know, there,

825
01:12:38,980 --> 01:12:41,601
this many people using this route or something like that.

826
01:12:41,621 --> 01:12:47,583
From a persuasion and communications point of view, it can have a lot of value.

827
01:12:48,143 --> 01:12:55,026
I think from that perspective as well, there's value to saying, we know that people aren't biking here.

828
01:12:55,986 --> 01:12:56,386
Why not?

829
01:12:56,406 --> 01:13:02,409
Obviously, they want to go this way though because there are things to do on this way, but they're not biking here.

830
01:13:03,549 --> 01:13:05,750
This is a route that needs improving because no one's biking on it.

831
01:13:06,305 --> 01:13:08,910
I think also, sorry, one second when I was doing it.

832
01:13:09,932 --> 01:13:10,513
I see Ernie's.

833
01:13:10,533 --> 01:13:11,455
Sorry, go ahead.

834
01:13:12,798 --> 01:13:13,018
Oh, no.

835
01:13:18,529 --> 01:13:41,669
I was just going to follow up Pat and saying when I spent a few days and bunches of hours watching High Street and West Medford as an example, what was really informative is to try to figure out in the future how a bike lane versus just shadows would work when collecting the observational data from how people drive.

836
01:13:42,269 --> 01:13:47,411
and where it would be impossible to try to squeeze them with a bike lane, et cetera.

837
01:13:48,031 --> 01:13:56,053
Curvy roads like that, you can't just assume people will drive in the way that the road suggests.

838
01:13:56,933 --> 01:14:08,597
So it was helpful to be able to do diligence on the safety of anything we would propose by actually going out and looking at it for a while.

839
01:14:08,617 --> 01:14:10,298
We should be careful about

840
01:14:12,078 --> 01:14:16,668
safety of paint because there are studies showing that shadows do nothing to improve safety.

841
01:14:17,008 --> 01:14:20,676
So Emily, I heard you before there.

842
01:14:21,718 --> 01:14:24,580
I was going to mention along the lines of what Pat said.

843
01:14:24,600 --> 01:14:34,325
I thought it was notable when I was doing counts in Medford Square that we all know that that's a complicated intersection.

844
01:14:35,085 --> 01:14:41,869
What I thought was the interesting observation I made, that was before I lived as close to Medford Square as I do now.

845
01:14:43,630 --> 01:15:02,060
but it was notable to me that for the fairly high number of people comparatively who were biking through Medford Square at rush hour, how few of them actually follow the established traffic patterns because it's really not a friendly thing to do if you're on a bike.

846
01:15:02,180 --> 01:15:08,243
Everybody has their own different way of picking their way through crosswalks and sidewalks and things.

847
01:15:08,684 --> 01:15:10,485
The fact that we can stand out there and say,

848
01:15:11,325 --> 01:15:25,894
there's this many people who need to get through this intersection and it really just does not work in its current configuration for these users, that's an interesting thing to be able to say.

849
01:15:25,934 --> 01:15:30,717
It does make sense to have spot observations like that.

850
01:15:33,478 --> 01:15:45,300
